    "findings": [
      {
        "check": "remaining_external_calls",
        "result": "Reviewed the discovered main contract, inherited ownership and reentrancy functions, and reachable transfer helpers. The additional manager calls collect already owed tokens or increase principal. Low-level token calls encode fixed ERC20 transfer, transferFrom or approve selectors; native-asset calls carry empty calldata. No arbitrary-calldata forwarding or delegatecall path was found. The ERC20 transfer selector is separately probed against the actual NFT manager."
      },
      {
        "check": "storage_writes",
        "result": "The existing record manager and NFT identity have no replacement setter. Record creation follows a successful incoming NFT transfer. Existing expiry updates require a larger date; record deletion follows the blocked withdrawal or migration path. Owner, pending owner, collectors and fee fields do not bypass the separately enforced date. Configuration writers are evaluated separately and excluded from the frozen-configuration metric."
      },
      {
        "check": "callbacks_and_modifiers",
        "result": "The source applies the shared reentrancy modifier to deposit, collect, increase, decrease, withdraw, relock, migration, record-owner changes and refunds. The modifier changes only its guard flag and checks it before entry. Fee/token/native callbacks do not gain a callable unguarded LP exit in the inspected source. This is a bounded source review, not a formal proof covering arbitrary external-contract bugs."
      },
      {
        "check": "operator_approvals",
        "result": "No setApprovalForAll or NFT permit forwarding path exists in the inspected custody source. The explicit NFT approval path has a false nonzero-address guard at the observed block. Per-NFT approval is checked as zero by RPC. Token approvals in the increase path apply to position tokens and follow incoming transfers, rather than granting an operator permission over the already held NFT."
      },
      {
        "check": "incoming_transfer_scope",
        "result": "For the already held NFT, the deposit path first requires transferring the same manager and token ID from msg.sender to the custodian. ownerOf identifies the custodian as current owner. No self-call with deposit calldata exists; guarded callbacks cannot reenter deposit. The later LP fee decrease applies to the same incoming NFT expressions. A real manager call with the custodian as caller and the record owner as from is probed separately."
      }
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "manual source review is required for this experiment",
      "one non-proxy EVM custodian and one NFT covering all pool principal",
      "does not establish immunity to future configuration changes or arbitrary undiscovered vulnerabilities"
    ]
  },
